now to a hereditary condition that  threatens many lives in africa people  with sickle cell disorder produce red  blood cells that are unusually shaped  like crescents or sickles as the name  suggests now these can block blood  vessels and cause chronic pain and often  death the condition is particularly  common among people with african or  caribbean background  now 300 000 children are born with  sickle cell disease each year with  around three quarters of those in africa  nigeria is the worst affected country  but there are attempts to sound the  alarm as dw correspondent olisa chukumar  reports  drawing blood to prevent immense  suffering nigeria's sickle cell  foundation a non-profit organization is  trying to test as many blood samples as  possible so people can know their sickle  cell status mudukwe ukola or lokwade  kopola is at the forefront of these  efforts  she's lived with sickle cell disorder  for more than 60 years  it was very excruciating  i was always in and out  of the hospital  for one thing or another  infection i have to be transfused  my primary school days my secondary  school days  were not very good it wasn't a good  experience  she calls herself a sickle cell warrior  braving every day with strong support  from her family and with medical care  but for a disease with no easy cure  she wants to raise awareness to prevent  the need for the lifelong challenges it  brings  it is indeed very important at least  for people to know  because if despite all the awareness a  lot of people still don't know  so this community program is very much  important and this foundation has been  doing a lot  about this over 40 million nigerians are  healthy carriers of the sickle cell gene  but some are not even aware of it the  disorder can only occur when two of such  people have children  one in four of their offspring will have  sickle cell anemia  which causes chronic pain and often  death  many don't make it beyond their fifth  birthday this particular event today now  gives us opportunity to do their blood  tests  so they know again  before they start dating and getting  married  what their genotype is because that's  what determines if you are going to be  if you are going to pass the gene of the  disease to your offspring this is  exactly what more and more couples are  starting to do it's often even a  decisive factor for getting together as  we hear at a wedding registrar next door  knowing  about sick self if i met my wife it's an  eye opener before i get married so that  i will know what i'm going into into and  how to avoid them  enlightenment is going to play a lot of  growth  in eradicating sickle cell so that you  don't have to reach children into the  world  to suffer  that suffering is worsened by the huge  financial costs and the challenges  accessing quality healthcare nigeria  pose that's why the campaigners are  advocating  know your status the cheaper and safer  message of prevention  we can now speak to dalali atigbe atipo  she is the chief operating officer of 54  gene which is a pharmaceutical research  company pioneering the specialization in  african genetic material delay it's good  to have you on the program now less than  three percent of genomic data  represented in researches from african  populations so how much has this tiny  amount hampered the search for a cure  for sickle cell disorder  well thank you it's greatly hampered the  search for a cure and when we think  about it what's really contributed to  that is the fact that we don't have much  representation  when it comes to driving the science we  need more african scientists to help  bridge the disparity gap when you think  about driving genomics data research so  that we have greater participation from  african science those who look like  those who are affected by the disease  contributing in addition we need to be  able to have greater investment when we  talk about diagnostics and data for  being able to identify  the causes 